git -C $csrbt commit -m "ADR-150: assigning a value is not typing -- the input no task could produce" `
  -m "The fifth page taken whole, and the first one that could not be driven. experiment-guide.html was the largest data-entry gap left at 16 of 52 fields -- the kit's design bench: a study builder that emits an .eco protocol, a benchmark pre-registration planner, a ten-item pre-flight checklist, and two LINTERS that say what is missing or weak in what you have designed. Three fields in, the task hit something no earlier page had: the linter says 'seed must be a whole number', and nothing the harness could do would make it say that. ASSIGNING A VALUE IS NOT TYPING. set-text writes through the value setter, which is what a SCRIPT does. A person presses keys, and for <input type=number> the two are not the same: assign `"3e`" and .value is `"`" with validity.badInput FALSE; type `"3e`" and .value is `"`" with badInput TRUE. A number box the browser cannot parse reads its value back as the empty string -- which is exactly what a deliberately blank box reads as -- and only badInput tells them apart, because badInput reflects the user's raw keystroke buffer, which no assignment fills. So a page that distinguishes them has a branch NO TASK IN THIS KIT COULD REACH. The new action type-text (DRAFT rung, the same kinds set-text takes) clicks the control, clears it, and sends real keystrokes. Chromium filters keystrokes on the way in, so not all text arrives: 'many' types as nothing at all and is not bad input, while '3e' is a number the user has started and not finished. What survives the filter is the browser's business; the harness types what a person types. THE DEFECT WAITING BEHIND IT. The guide's linter guarded the seed with ``value !== `"`"`` so that a BLANK seed could mean 'use 42' -- and a seed the user typed and the browser rejected reads as `"`" too. The box showed their text, the emitted protocol carried seed 42, and the linter said nothing. Fixed with validity.badInput, which is the interactive lab's own rule (ADR-148's numField: reported, never guessed) reaching the page next door. Keys and window were never silent -- they read as 'out of range' -- but that is a different sentence from the true one, and they now say 'is not a whole number' when that is what happened. THE TASK goes 42 steps to 165: 16 -> 52 OF 52 FIELDS, 53 -> 220 confirmed expectations, and the kit 349 -> 385 of 520, 67% -> 74%. Five pages are entered whole: collection sheet (59 of 63, four reagent rows deliberately blank), stand sheet, ecology lab, releve and experiment guide. THE GUIDE'S ADVICE IS CHECKABLE -- the linters are the page's product and nothing was checking them. The task now holds that EVERY FINDING IS ANSWERABLE and answering it clears EXACTLY that finding and no other: the engineering track opens with four MISSING (no question, no floor, no decision rule, no sizes) and each field filled removes its own line, ending at CLEAN -- pre-registration complete; the ecology track opens with two WEAK (no phases, no pre-registered hypotheses), a hypothesis naming a community that does not exist is WEAK and will grade UNGRADEABLE, and one comparing a simulated phase to entered field data is WEAK because they share no species. THREE MORE CLAIMS, NOW HELD. Two doors, one rule: the BUILDER refuses to add a second community with a name already taken -- the add is declined and the list does not grow -- while the IMPORTER accepts a protocol that has one and the linter reports it, because rewriting someone else's record on the way in would be changing it to make it pass. A verdict needs a rule AND numbers: a verdict without a measured row is refused for the same reason a verdict without a decision rule is, since a verdict is a reading against a bar and half of it is not a verdict. And a qualitative hypothesis is graded against a BAND rather than a number: choosing 'evenness ... is ...' puts away the Test and Value boxes and offers a Band list, and the emitted line reads 'expect: evenness(graze) is uneven' -- a prediction that names a band is falsifiable too. verify_report 95 (+10): the assign/type table, that typing an empty value CLEARS the control rather than doing nothing, and that typing into a button is refused the same way set-text refuses it. mutate_report 57 (+5), and the walk re-run across all 41 pages plus the two whole-kit sessions (21 -> 22 tools). THREE THINGS THE ROBOT FOUND THAT THE TASK COULD NOT. A CLICK IS NOT A FOCUS: type-text v1 clicked the control first, the way a person does, and nine walk steps across five pages timed out at 30 s on controls that are on the page but not clickable at that instant -- a person waits for the button; the harness must not. It focuses instead, and a control that cannot take focus is refused AT ONCE as a conflict rather than waited on. A FAILED FOCUS IS NOT A DEAD PAGE: v2 raised unavailable, which is fatal to a walk session, and four pages reported the page had gone away when only one control was unreachable. AN ACTION ADDED TO A POOL COMPETES FOR IT: type-text was put in the picker-search pool, and the walk of one page then drove type-text where it used to drive pick and reported pick undriven on a page that offers one -- the pool is a budget, not a list. And adding a 22nd tool re-rolls every seeded walk in the kit, so a page whose coverage depended on the old roll has to be re-walked and its seed stated. Held: type-text is slower than set-text -- a click, two key presses and one keystroke per character -- so it is for the cases where the difference matters rather than a replacement, and every existing task keeps set-text; it does not reproduce every difference between a script and a person, since a paste, an IME, a drag-select, an autofill and a keyboard repeat are all still outside it, and what it buys is one specific checkable distinction the pages themselves already act on; and the other number inputs across the kit have NOT been retyped, so anywhere a page treats a blank field as meaningful the same silence is possible and nothing here has looked -- that is a worklist, stated rather than done. deployment-log at 17 of 37 is now the largest gap left." `
